  "enhanced_information": "The patient presents with sudden severe epigastric pain and elevated lipase levels, indicating possible acute pancreatitis. Given the elevated alanine aminotransferase (ALT), there is a concern for biliary involvement, such as gallstones, which can cause pancreatitis. The most appropriate next step in management is a right upper quadrant abdominal ultrasound, as it is a non-invasive method to assess for gallstones and biliary obstruction. This aligns with the consensus that ultrasound is the first-line imaging modality in suspected cases of acute pancreatitis with potential gallbladder disease. Other imaging options, such as CT scans, are typically reserved for cases where complications are suspected or when ultrasound results are inconclusive.",
